---
// Lesson 6.3 — Jump Feel: Fall Speed & Coyote Time
// Asymmetric gravity, jump buffering, coyote time — feel engineering.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>6.3 — Jump Feel: Fall Speed &amp; Coyote Time</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your jump works (<a href="6.2.html">6.2</a>) but it feels like a
    spreadsheet. Nobody can say WHY — until you learn that "feel" is
    three tunables with names: asymmetric gravity, jump buffer, and
    coyote time. Today you make a jump that players call "tight"
    without knowing they were taught to.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Feel rule #1 — fall faster than you rise.</strong> Real
    games multiply gravity while falling. New code, same voice:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float fallMultiplier = 2.5f;

private void FixedUpdate()
{
    if (isPlatformer &amp;&amp; rb.velocity.y &lt; 0)
    {
        // falling: pull harder
        rb.gravityScale = baseGravity * fallMultiplier;
    }
    else if (rb.velocity.y &gt; 0)
    {
        rb.gravityScale = baseGravity; // rising: normal
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Save baseGravity in Start (read rb.gravityScale once). Now
        rise is a balloon and fall is a decision. Try it with
        fallMultiplier 1: the floaty "moon hop" you tuned away by hand
        in <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a> — this one knob replaces three
        fiddly jumpForce settings, and it is why nearly every
        platformer you love does NOT obey real physics.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Feel rule #2 — forgive the player.</strong> Two
    classic mercies, both timers (the
    <a href="3.3.html">3.3</a> pattern, pointed at kindness):</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Name</th><th>The complaint it kills</th><th>Implementation</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Coyote time</td><td>"I jumped but the ledge ate it"</td><td>remember the last grounded moment; allow jump for ~0.1s after</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jump buffer</td><td>"I pressed too early, nothing happened"</td><td>remember the last Space press; fire the jump the instant you land</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Both mercies are the SAME pattern: remember an event's
            timestamp, compare against Time.time, act on the gap.
            Why does this NOT make the game easier — what is the
            difference between forgiving the player's intent and
            playing for them?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — coyote time.</strong> Two fields and one
    comparison, added to the ground check from
    <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a>:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float coyoteTime = 0.1f;
private float lastGroundedTime;

// inside Update, right after isGrounded is set:
if (isGrounded) lastGroundedTime = Time.time;

// and the jump condition becomes:
if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space)
    &amp;&amp; Time.time - lastGroundedTime &lt;= coyoteTime)
{
    rb.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
    lastGroundedTime = -999f; // spend the coyote
}</code></pre>
    <p>Test the exact way players fail: run off a ledge and tap Space
    a beat late — you jump! Set coyoteTime to 0: the complaint is
    back. Set it to 0.5: you can fly off ledges for half a second —
    tune until the mercy is invisible.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — jump buffer.</strong> The mirror image:
    remember when the key was pressed, allow the jump shortly
    after landing:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float jumpBuffer = 0.12f;
private float lastJumpPressTime;

if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space)) lastJumpPressTime = Time.time;

if (isGrounded &amp;&amp; Time.time - lastJumpPressTime &lt;= jumpBuffer)
{
    rb.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
    lastJumpPressTime = -999f;
}</code></pre>
    <p>Test: stand under a low ceiling... no — simpler: press Space
    a hair before you land from a jump. Before the buffer: nothing,
    press again. After: the jump just HAPPENS. Your inputs feel
    honored.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The mushy jump</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Forget to spend the coyote (no lastGroundedTime =
                -999 after the jump). Jump once in place, repeatedly.
                What does Time.time - lastGroundedTime equal while
                you are STILL on the ground — and why does that mean
                the mercy never expires?</li>
            <li>Coyote time lets you jump from a SPIKE's edge after
                you already left ground. Trace lastGroundedTime
                through the death (<a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>) —
                why does respawn reset the mercy, and should it?</li>
            <li>Buffer 0.12 with a LOW jump means double-fire chains:
                land → buffer fires → land again... how would the
                -999 spend stop this on the SECOND landing?</li>
            <li>Set fallMultiplier to 10. The character lands like a
                dropped anvil — fun? For which genre would 10 be
                correct (one-word answer: a ___ game like Downwell)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Feel portfolio</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Tune a "heavy knight": fallMultiplier 3, coyote 0.08,
                buffer 0.08. Then a "nimble rogue": 1.8, 0.15, 0.2.
                Swap a partner between yours and theirs — can they
                feel the class you built without being told?</li>
            <li>Write your three numbers on paper and hand them to
                another team — rebuild from paper only. Are the
                numbers a program? (AP exam word: abstraction — what
                do the numbers HIDE?)</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"6.3a: fall multiplier + coyote + buffer"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain asymmetric gravity and wire fallMultiplier in FixedUpdate</li>
            <li>Build coyote time and jump buffer from the timestamp pattern</li>
            <li>Explain "spending" a grace period and what double-fire is</li>
            <li>Defend mercies as intent-forgiveness, not difficulty-lowering</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/index.html">Unit 6: Jumping</a> — the full feel guide</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — velocity, Time.time patterns</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="6.4.html">6.4 — Boots: Double Jump</a></p>
</div>`;
